🚦 What is ELB?

Elastic Load Balancer is a managed AWS service that automatically distributes incoming network traffic across multiple targets — like EC2 instances, containers, or IP addresses — in one or more Availability Zones (AZs).

It's like a traffic controller that ensures no single resource is overwhelmed and helps scale your applications reliably.

📚 Types of Load Balancers

AWS provides 3 main types of load balancers under ELB:

Application Load Balancer (ALB) ---------- (mostly used)
Operates at Layer 7 (HTTP/HTTPS).
Best for web applications.
Supports:
Path-based routing (/images, /api)
Host-based routing (app.example.com, api.example.com)
WebSocket support
Redirects (HTTP → HTTPS)
Integration with WAF (Web Application Firewall)
Network Load Balancer (NLB)
Works at Layer 4 (TCP/UDP).
Super high performance and low latency.
Supports:
Millions of requests per second
Static IPs and Elastic IPs
TLS termination
Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB) -------- (least used)
Used to deploy, scale, and manage third-party virtual appliances (firewalls, monitoring tools, etc.).
Routes all traffic to these appliances using GENEVE protocol.
Best for security and inspection use cases.
🎯 Target Types

Each ELB routes traffic to targets registered in target groups:

EC2 Instances
IP addresses (even outside AWS)
Lambda functions (only with ALB)
Containers (ECS tasks)
🛠️ How ELB Works (Step by Step)

A client sends a request (e.g., a browser hits https://myapp.com)
DNS (Route 53 or others) resolves the domain to the ELB DNS name
The ELB receives the request
ELB evaluates routing rules (e.g., /api → target group A)
ELB picks a healthy target using load balancing algorithm (e.g., round robin)
ELB forwards the request to the target
The target processes the request and sends the response back through the ELB to the client
🧠 Features of ELB

Auto Scaling integration: Automatically handles increase/decrease in traffic
Health checks: Monitors instance health; only routes to healthy ones
SSL termination: Decrypt HTTPS at ELB, reducing backend CPU load
Sticky sessions: Keeps users connected to the same instance (ALB/NLB)
Cross-zone load balancing: Distributes traffic evenly across AZs
Access logs: Store request logs in S3
Security groups (for ALB): Acts as a virtual firewall
🔐 Security with ELB

Security Groups (for ALB): Controls inbound/outbound traffic
TLS Certificates (HTTPS): Use AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)
WAF (Web Application Firewall): Protects against common web threats (with ALB)
💸 Pricing

ELB charges are based on:

Hours used
GB of data processed
(And for ALB: number of LCUs — Load Balancer Capacity Units)
🧩 Use Cases

Distribute web traffic to multiple EC2s (web server farm)
Serve microservices (via ALB path routing)
Handle massive TCP/UDP traffic (via NLB)
Route to serverless backends (ALB → Lambda)
Security scanning with Gateway Load Balancer
🟦 Steps to Create an Application Load Balancer (ALB)

Go to EC2 Dashboard > Load Balancers
Click Create Load Balancer
Choose Application Load Balancer
Configure Basic Settings
Name your ALB
Scheme: Internet-facing or Internal
IP address type: IPv4 or Dualstack (IPv4 & IPv6)
Configure Listeners and AZs
Listener: default is HTTP (port 80) or HTTPS (443) if you have a certificate
Choose at least 2 Availability Zones and corresponding subnets
Configure Security Groups
Select or create a security group allowing HTTP (80) or HTTPS (443)
Configure Routing
Create a Target Group:
Type: Instance, IP, or Lambda
Protocol: HTTP
Health check: define the health check path (like /health)
Register Targets
Select EC2 instances to attach to this target group
Review & Create
Review all settings and click Create Load Balancer
🟩 Steps to Create a Network Load Balancer (NLB)

Go to EC2 Dashboard > Load Balancers
Click Create Load Balancer
Choose Network Load Balancer
Configure Basic Settings
Name the NLB
Scheme: Internet-facing or Internal
IP type: IPv4 or Dualstack
Configure Listeners and AZs
Listener protocol: TCP or UDP
Add Availability Zones and subnets
You can also allocate Elastic IPs (optional)
Configure Target Group
Create a target group:
Type: Instance or IP
Protocol: TCP, UDP, or TLS
Port and health check settings (defaults to TCP)
Register Targets
Add EC2 instances manually or automatically via IP
Review & Create
Confirm everything, then click Create Load Balancer
ALB vs NLB: Comparison Table

Feature	ALB (Application Load Balancer)	NLB (Network Load Balancer)
Protocol Support	HTTP, HTTPS	TCP, UDP, TLS
Layer	Layer 7 (Application Layer)	Layer 4 (Transport Layer)
Routing Features	Advanced: Path-based, host-based, query params	Basic: IP + Port based
Latency	Slightly higher (because of Layer 7 processing)	Ultra-low latency (optimized for speed)
TLS Termination	Yes	Optional (can pass-through or terminate)
WebSocket Support	Yes	Yes
Target Types	Instance, IP, Lambda	Instance, IP, ALB
Health Checks	HTTP/HTTPS based (URL/path)	TCP-based (basic port check)
Use Case Focus	Content-based routing, web apps, microservices	High-performance, real-time apps, gaming, IoT
Pricing	Charged per hour + LCU	Charged per hour + data processed
🟦 ALB Use Cases & Examples

🧠 Use Case 1: Web Application with Path-based Routing

Example: yourdomain.com/api, yourdomain.com/login, yourdomain.com/dashboard
ALB can route traffic to different target groups based on the path.
🧠 Use Case 2: Host-based Routing for Multi-tenant Apps

Example: admin.example.com → Admin service, user.example.com → User service
🧠 Use Case 3: Microservices Architecture

ALB works well with ECS/EKS and Lambda functions.
📝 ALB Ideal for: Modern web applications, REST APIs, serverless apps, containerized apps

🟩 NLB Use Cases & Examples

⚡ Use Case 1: Real-Time Applications

Example: Online gaming, VoIP, chat apps needing low latency
NLB’s TCP/UDP support and fast connection handling are a good fit
⚡ Use Case 2: Static IP Needs / Legacy Systems

NLB can be assigned Elastic IPs for firewall rules or DNS mapping
⚡ Use Case 3: TLS Passthrough

If you want the backend service to handle decryption (not the load balancer), NLB supports TLS passthrough
📝 NLB Ideal for: Financial apps, gaming, IoT, low-latency or network-level routing use cases

🎧 What is a Listener in ELB?

A listener is like a "doorman" for your load balancer.

It checks for incoming traffic on a specific port and protocol, and then routes that traffic to your backend targets (like EC2 instances, containers, or Lambda functions).

It lets you define what kind of request should go to which target group.

🔹 Components of a Listener

Each listener has:

1. Protocol – What kind of traffic to listen for (e.g., HTTP, HTTPS, TCP, TLS) 2. Port – The port number the load balancer listens on (e.g., 80 for HTTP, 443 for HTTPS) 3. Rules (mainly for ALB) – Define how to route traffic based on conditions (like path, host) 4. Target Group – Where the traffic should be forwarded

Protocol – What kind of traffic to listen for (e.g., HTTP, HTTPS, TCP, TLS)
Port – The port number the load balancer listens on (e.g., 80 for HTTP, 443 for HTTPS)
Rules (mainly for ALB) – Define how to route traffic based on conditions (like path, host)
Target Group – Where the traffic should be forwarded
🧠 Example Scenarios

🎯 Example 1: ALB Listener for a Web App

Listener:
Protocol: HTTP
Port: 80
Rules:
/api/* → Target Group A (API servers)
/app/* → Target Group B (Frontend servers)
🎯 Example 2: NLB Listener for a TCP App

Listener:
Protocol: TCP
Port: 443
Target Group: EC2 instances running a secure app on port 443
🔄 Listener Rules (Only for ALB)

Listener rules allow you to:

Match requests based on:
URL path
Hostname
HTTP headers or query strings
Then forward to different target groups
⚠️ NLB and CLB do not support these advanced rules — they only forward traffic based on port and protocol.

🏷️ Common Protocols Used in Listeners

ELB Type	Supported Protocols
ALB	HTTP, HTTPS
NLB	TCP, UDP, TLS
CLB	HTTP, HTTPS, TCP, SSL
✅ Summary

Term	Meaning
Listener	Entry point to ELB; listens on a protocol and port
Rules	Define how to forward traffic (only for ALB)
Target Group	Group of backend servers that receive the forwarded traffic
